                            <li><a href="#12">Step 12: Be Long-term and Patient</a></li>
                            <li><a href="#13">Final Thoughts</a></li>

                        </div>
                        <p class="span">In the present-day competitive environment of SEO, it is no longer sufficient to be ranked by random keywords. <strong>Topical authority</strong> is now a priority of the search engines, which are websites that demonstrate consistent, deep expertise over a topic. To have long-term positions, stable traffic, and increased conversions, establishing topical authority is necessary.</p>
                        <p class="span">This step by step guide will demonstrate <strong>how to build a topical authority for your website</strong> in a systematic and sustainable manner.</p>

                        <h4 class="text-tranf" id="1">Step 1: Learn What Topical Authority is All About</h3>
                        <p class="span">Topical authority is the perceived authority of your site in a certain niche or in a particular subject.</p>

                        <p class="span"><b>Search engines evaluate:</b></p>
                        <li>Content depth and relevance</li>
                        <li>Internal linking structure</li>
                        <li>Coverage of subtopics</li>
                        <li>User engagement signals</li>
                        <li>Consistency of publishing</li>
                        <p class="span">A topical authority site does not simply answer a single question but answers all the significant questions concerning a topic.</p><br>

                        <h4 class="text-tranf" id="2">Step 2: Select a Single Core Topic (Do Not Go Broa too Early)</h4>
                        <p class="span">Among the most common errors that the websites commit is writing on too many irrelevant subjects.</p>

                        <p class="span"><b>To build topical authority:</b></p>
                        <p class="span">Select a single key subject (i.e. SEO, digital marketing, hair transplant, astrology).</p>

                        <p class="span"><b>Ensure the topic aligns with:</b></p>
                        <li>Your business/service</li>
                        <li>Audience intent</li>
                        <li>Long-term content potential</li><br>

                        <p class="span"><b>Example:</b></p>
                        <p class="span">Rather than composing about SEO, social media and web design, begin with <a href="https://www.kingofdigitalmarketing.com/SEO-Services.aspx">SEO.</a></p><br>

                        <h4 class="text-tranf" id="3">Step 3: Topical Keyword Research (Keywords, not primary keywords)</h4>
                        <p class="span">The classical search of the key word deals with individual keywords. There must be <strong>topic-based mapping of keywords,</strong> topical authority.</p>
                        
                        <p class="span"><b>Do the following:</b></p>
                        <p class="span">Identifty one pillar keyword.</p>

                        <p class="span"><b>Find:</b></p>
                        <li>Supporting keywords</li>
                        <li>Long-tail Keywords</li>
                        <li>Informational queries</li>
                        <li>Transactional queries</li><br>
                        
                        <p class="span"><b>Example for SEO topic:</b></p>
                        <li>What is SEO?</li>
                        <li>On-page SEO checklist</li>
                        <li>Technical SEO basics</li>
                        <li>Internal linking strategy</li>
                        <li>SEO tools comparison</li>
                        <p class="span">This is what builds out a <strong>content ecosystem,</strong> as opposed to individual posts.</p><br>
                        
                        <h4 class="text-tranf" id="4">Step 4: Develop Pillar Pages (Background Content)</p>
                        <p class="span">This is what builds out a <strong>content ecosystem,</strong> as opposed to individual posts.</p>

                        <p class="span"><b>These are the features of a good pillar page:</b></p>
                        <li>2000-3000 words</li>
                        <li>Discusses all key subtopics in a nutshell</li>
                        <li>Connection with elaborate support articles</li>
                        <li>Optimized on a high volume keyword</li><br>

                        <p class="span"><b>Example pillar:</b></p>
                        <li>Total Guide to <a href="https://www.kingofdigitalmarketing.com/SEO-Services.aspx">Search Engine Optimization.</a></li>
                        <li>This page is the source of authority over your topic.</li><br>

                        <h4 class="text-tranf" id="5">Step 5: Establish Pillar Page Topic Clusters</h4>
                        <p class="span">The detailed articles that explore in-depth all the subtopics are known as topic clusters.</p>

                        <p class="span"><b>Each cluster article should:</b></p>
                        <li>Consider one particular subtopic</li>
                        <li>Answer user intent clearly</li>
                        <li>Link back to the pillar page</li>
                        <li>Connect between relational cluster posts</li><br>
                        
                        <p class="span"><b>Example clusters:</b></p>
                        <li>How to optimize meta tags</li>
                        <li><a href="https://www.kingofdigitalmarketing.com/blog/image-seo-ranking-factors-that-actually-matter.aspx">Image SEO ranking factors</a></li>
                        <li>Search intent optimization</li>
                        <li>Best practices of structuring URLs</li><br>
                        
                        <p class="span"><b>These internal linkages inform the search engines:</b></p>
                        <li>The site is completely in possession of this subject.</li><br>
                        

                        <h4 class="text-tranf" id="6">Step 6: Strategic Internal Linkage</h4>
                        <p class="span">Topical authority relies upon internal linking.</p>

                        <p class="span"><b>Best practices:</b></p>
                        <li>Connect old pages with new ones, having a higher authority.</li>
                        <li>Make natural, descriptive anchor text.</li>
                        <li>Avoid over-optimization.</li>
                        <li>Link all the significant pages.</li><br>
                        
                        <p class="span"><b>Internal links help:</b></p>
                        <li>Distribute link equity</li>
                        <li>Improve crawlability</li>
                        <li>Establish topic relevance</li><br>
                        
                        <h4 class="text-tranf" id="7">Step 7: Search Intent Equilibrium at All Levels</h4>
                        <p class="span">Topical authority cannot work when content does not correspond to intent.</p>

                        <p class="span"><b>There are 4 main intents:</b></p>
                        <li>Informational (learn)</li>
                        <li>Navigational (find)</li>
                        <li>Commercial (compare)</li>
                        <li>Transactional (buy)</li>
                        <li>The articles must have a clear focus in each.</li><br>
                        
                        <p class="span"><b>Example:</b></p>
                        <li><strong>"What is topical authority?"</strong> - Informational</li>
                        <li>"The top 10 SEO tools: beginners guide - Business.</li>
                        <li><a href="https://www.kingofdigitalmarketing.com/SEO-Package.aspx">SEO services pricing</a> - Transactional</li>
                        <li>These are intent alignment and better rankings and user trust</li><br>

                        <h4 class="text-tranf" id="8">Step 8: Ensure Content Consistency and Dept</h4><br>
                       
                        <p class="span"><b>The search engines have confidence with websites which:</b></p>
                        <li>Publish regularly</li>
                        <li>Update old content</li>
                        <li>Cover topics in depth</li>
                        <li>There should be no skinny or duplicate pages.</li><br>
                        
                        <p class="span"><b>Best practices:</b></p>
                        <li>Revise articles on a biannual or yearly basis.</li>
                        <li>Add new insights and data.</li>
                        <li>Remove outdated content.</li>
                        <li>Merge overlapping articles.</li>
                        <li>Credibility is created through the use of consistency.</li><br>

                        <h4 class="text-tranf" id="9">Step 9: Enhance E-E-A-T Signals</h4><br>

                        <p class="span"><b>Topical authority is directly associated with E-E-A-T:</b></p>
                        <li>Experience</li>
                        <li>Expertise</li>
                        <li>Authoritativeness</li>
                        <li>Trustworthiness</li><br>
                        
                        <p class="span"><b>Ways to improve:</b></p>
                        <li>Add author bios</li>
                        <li>Showcase experience</li>
                        <li>Include real examples</li>
                        <li>Cite reliable sources</li>
                        <li>Keep About and Contact pages open.</li>
                        <li>Google does not favor anonymous content or even fake professionals.</li><br>

                        <h4 class="text-tranf" id="10">Step 10: Develop External Signals (However, Do not rely on Links Only)</h4>
                        <p class="span">Backinks are welcome, but first there is topical authority.</p>
                        
                        <p class="span"><b>Focus on:</b></p>
                        <li>Contributions to niche websites.</li>
                        <li>Brand mentions</li>
                        <li>Content shares</li>
                        <li>Natural link acquisition</li>
                        <li>The ecosystem is well-structured which makes links natural.</li><br>

                        <h4 class="text-tranf" id="11">Step 11: Topical Authority Growth Measurement</h4>

                        <p class="span"><b>Track progress using:</b></p>
                        <li>Keyword coverage expansion</li>
                        <li>Organic traffic growth</li>
                        <li>Time on page</li>
                        <li>Internal link performance</li>
                        <li>Ranking stability amongst related keywords.</li>
                        <li>Topical authority is not an overnight affair--it builds up.</li><br>

                        <h4 class="text-tranf" id="12">Step 12: Be Long-term and Patient</h4>
                        <p class="span">Topical authority is an <a href="https://www.kingofdigitalmarketing.com/blog/how-to-master-seo-in-just-30-days.aspx">SEO strategy</a> that cannot be achieved in the short term.</p>
                        
                        <p class="span"><b>The results tend to be given after:</b></p>
                        <li>3-6 months (early signals)</li>
                        <li>6-12 months (strong authority)</li>
                        <li>12+ months (dominant presence)</li>
                        <li>Consistency outwits frugality in all aspects.</li><br>
                        
                        <h4 class="text-tranf" id="13">Final Thoughts</h4>
                        <p class="span"><strong>Topical authority</strong> is what you need in order to have sustainable rankings, better conversions, and algorithm-proof SEO.</p>

                        <p class="span">Rather than pursuing keywords, own topics. Rankings naturally follow when search engines consider your site as the best one in relation to the information on a topic.</p>

